The mechanics, verified:

```js
// Chaining — each .then gets the previous result
Promise.resolve(1)
  .then(v => v + 1)      // 2
  .then(v => v * 10)     // 20
  .then(v => console.log(v));  // chain: 20 ✓

// Each .then returns a NEW promise (not the same one)
const p2 = Promise.resolve('a');
const t1 = p2.then(() => 'b');
// t1 !== p2 — true (verified). Chains are pipelines, not broadcasts.

// Errors skip fulfilled handlers, propagate to nearest .catch
Promise.reject(new Error('boom'))
  .then(() => console.log('skipped'))     // skipped
  .catch(e => console.log(e.message))      // 'boom' ✓
  .then(() => 'catch recovers — chain continues');  // ✓

// .finally runs regardless, passes the value through
Promise.resolve(42).finally(cleanup).then(v => v);  // still 42 ✓
```

The state machine (the interview core):
- pending → fulfilled (via resolve) or pending → rejected (via reject)
- Settled is permanent: resolve-then-reject does nothing; a fulfilled promise never re-fires .then
- Handlers attach anytime: before or after settlement — late subscribers to an already-settled promise fire as microtasks immediately

The chaining rules that follow:
- Returning a value in .then → next .then receives it
- Returning a PROMISE in .then → the chain waits for it, then unwraps (the flat-map behavior — no .then nesting)
- Throwing in .then → the chain rejects at that point, skipping to the next .catch

The constructor (where promises are born):
```js
new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  // the executor runs SYNCHRONOUSLY
  doAsync((err, data) => err ? reject(err) : resolve(data));
});
```
